comments.body:
@petyaslavova a short summary, since the bot rounds changed the approach twice. As of 5d4f7c24, `release()` keeps the connection in the in-use set while the release listeners run, runs them without the pool lock (a `MOVING` notification read during the `AUTH` reply takes that lock), and then makes the connection available under the lock, running the listeners again if `re_auth_callback()` queued a new token meanwhile.

Two trade-offs I chose, happy to change either:
- the `_re_auth_queued` set tracks connections that got a new token during their release; checking the connection's own pending token instead would drop the set if you prefer fewer moving parts;
- the listeners can run a second time in that case, instead of applying the token later.

One gap that predates this PR and isn't changed here: `re_auth()` clears `_re_auth_token` after reading the reply, so a token that arrives while a previous `AUTH` reply is being read is dropped. I can follow up on that separately if useful.
